.TH "DOCUMENTFRAGMENT.MOVEBEFORE" "3JS" "September 12, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
DocumentFragment.moveBefore \- DocumentFragment: moveBefore() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]moveBefore()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]DocumentFragment\f[R] interface moves a given \f[CR]Node\f[R]
inside the invoking \f[CR]DocumentFragment\f[R] as a direct child,
before a given reference node.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
moveBefore(movedNode, referenceNode)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]movedNode\f[R]
A \f[CR]Node\f[R] representing the node to be moved.
Note that this must be an \f[CR]Element\f[R] or a
\f[CR]CharacterData\f[R] node.
.TP
\f[B]referenceNode\f[R]
A \f[CR]Node\f[R] that \f[CR]movedNode\f[R] will be moved before, or
\f[CR]null\f[R].
If the value is \f[CR]null\f[R], \f[CR]movedNode\f[R] is inserted at the
end of the invoking \f[CR]DocumentFragment\f[R]\(cqs child nodes.
.SS Return value
None (\f[CR]undefined\f[R]).
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]HierarchyRequestError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown in any of the following situations:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The fragment and \f[CR]movedNode\f[R] have different shadow\-including
roots (the roots returned by
\f[CR]getRootNode({ composed: true })\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
The move would place a node inside itself or one of its descendants,
including through a shadow tree.
.IP \(bu 2
The specified \f[CR]movedNode\f[R] is not an \f[CR]Element\f[R] or
\f[CR]CharacterData\f[R] node.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]NotFoundError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
The specified \f[CR]referenceNode\f[R] is not a child of the
\f[CR]DocumentFragment\f[R] you are calling \f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R] on,
that is, the fragment you are trying to move \f[CR]movedNode\f[R]
inside.
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R]
The second argument was not supplied.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R] method moves a given node to a new place in
the \f[CR]DocumentFragment\f[R].
It provides similar functionality to the \f[CR]Node.insertBefore()\f[R]
method, except that it doesn\(cqt remove and then reinsert the node.
This means that the state of the node (which would be reset if moving it
with \f[CR]insertBefore()\f[R] and similar mechanisms) is preserved
after the move.
This includes:
.IP \(bu 2
Animation and transition state.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<iframe>\f[R] loading state.
.IP \(bu 2
Interactivity states (for example, \f[CR]:focus\f[R] and
\f[CR]:active\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
Fullscreen element state.
.IP \(bu 2
Open/close state of popovers.
.IP \(bu 2
Modal state of \f[CR]<dialog>\f[R] elements (modal dialogs will not be
closed).
.PP
The play state of \f[CR]<video>\f[R] and \f[CR]<audio>\f[R] elements is
not included in the above list, as these elements retain their state
when removed and reinserted, regardless of the mechanism used.
.PP
An ordinary \f[CR]DocumentFragment\f[R] is detached.
Appending it to the document transfers its children and leaves the
fragment empty; it does not connect the fragment itself.
The state\-preserving behavior is therefore most useful on a connected
\f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R], which inherits from \f[CR]DocumentFragment\f[R].
.PP
When observing changes to the DOM using a \f[CR]MutationObserver\f[R],
nodes moved with \f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R] will be recorded with a removed
node and an added node.
.SS \f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R] constraints
There are some constraints to be aware of when using
\f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R]:
.IP \(bu 2
It can only work when moving a node within the same shadow\-including
root.
For an ordinary detached fragment, this means moving nodes already
within that fragment.
For a connected shadow root, nodes connected to the same document meet
this requirement.
.IP \(bu 2
It won\(cqt work if you try to move a node that is not connected to the
DOM to an already connected parent, or vice versa.
.PP
In such cases, \f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R] will fail with a
\f[CR]HierarchyRequestError\f[R] exception.
If the above constraints are requirements for your particular use case,
you should use \f[CR]Node.insertBefore()\f[R] instead, or use
\f[CR]try...catch\f[R] to handle the errors that arise from such cases.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Preserving focus inside a shadow root
This example demonstrates how \f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R] preserves an
input\(cqs focus when moving it inside a shadow root.
.SS HTML
The HTML contains a \f[CR]<div>\f[R] to host the shadow root and an
\f[CR]<output>\f[R] to display whether the input remains focused after
each move.
.IP
.EX
<div id=\(dqhost\(dq></div>
<output id=\(dqstatus\(dq></output>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The script attaches a shadow root to the \f[CR]<div>\f[R] and adds an
input and a paragraph to it.
The input\(cqs \f[CR]keydown\f[R] event listener uses
\f[CR]moveBefore()\f[R] to move the input after the paragraph by passing
\f[CR]null\f[R], or before it by passing the paragraph as the reference
node.
After each move, it updates the status output with whether the input is
focused.
.IP
.EX
const shadow = document.getElementById(\(dqhost\(dq).attachShadow({ mode: \(dqopen\(dq });
const status = document.getElementById(\(dqstatus\(dq);
const input = document.createElement(\(dqinput\(dq);
input.setAttribute(\(dqaria\-label\(dq, \(dqType here, then press Enter to move\(dq);
input.placeholder = \(dqPress Enter to move\(dq;
const paragraph = document.createElement(\(dqp\(dq);
paragraph.textContent = \(dqThe input can move before or after this paragraph.\(dq;
shadow.append(input, paragraph);

input.addEventListener(\(dqkeydown\(dq, (event) => {
  if (event.key !== \(dqEnter\(dq || event.isComposing) {
    return;
  }
  event.preventDefault();
  const reference = input.nextSibling === paragraph ? null : paragraph;
  shadow.moveBefore(input, reference);
  status.textContent = \(gaInput still focused: ${shadow.activeElement === input}\(ga;
});
.EE
.SS Result
Focus the input and press Enter to move it before or after the
paragraph.
The input remains focused.
.PP
Using \f[CR]shadow.insertBefore(input, reference)\f[R] instead would
remove and reinsert the input, losing its focus.
Ordinary reinsertion preserves the text inside the input box, but not
the other states listed above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Document.moveBefore()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.moveBefore()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Node.insertBefore()\f[R]
